.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_PAUSE 3 "2026-07-18" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_pause \- temporarily stop delivering events for an fd registration, without destroying it
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_event_loop_pause(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function works on an fd only. A call on a registration that comes
from a queue selectable or a ccol_channel selectable returns
ccol_invalid_args. ccol_event_loop_modify has the same restriction.

ccol_event_loop_remove fully unregisters reg, and it defers reg for a
later free. ccol_event_loop_pause is different. It leaves reg fully
intact. reg still holds its slot on the shared epoll entry of the fd
below it. It still counts toward ccol_event_loop_reg_count. It still
carries the same ccol_event_loop_reg_generation.

This function recomputes the combined epoll interest mask of the fd so
that the mask excludes reg. The fd may carry another direction. If
that other direction is itself live and not paused, this is a single
EPOLL_CTL_MOD call. If every direction on the fd is now paused, the
library removes the fd from the epoll interest set of the kernel
completely. The fd then produces no wake-up of any kind for as long as
it stays fully paused. This covers its own error condition and its own
hangup condition. The kernel reports those whatever interest mask you
ask for. No on_readable, on_writable or on_error callback fires for
reg while it is paused. The other direction on the same fd, if there
is one, is unaffected.

This call is the cheap alternative to a ccol_event_loop_remove and a
later ccol_event_loop_add. It suits a caller pattern where the same
logical registration comes back. One example is a connection that goes
to a worker thread for blocking body I/O, and then comes back to the
reactor for its next request. A fresh ccol_event_loop_add and
ccol_event_loop_remove pair costs one heap allocation and one free,
plus churn in the chmap of the fd registry. Pause and resume avoid
both completely.

With more than one reactor thread, EPOLLONESHOT is in play. That is
the case where num_reactor_threads > 1. A dispatch worker then runs
its own re-arm one more time after a paused callback returns. This
causes no problem. A re-arm always calculates the epoll mask from the
current state. The extra re-arm therefore applies the same mask, which
does not include reg. It does not cause a race. In this case too, pause
and resume prevent the allocation and the changes to the registry.

A pause of a reg that is already paused succeeds and does nothing.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  ccol_event_loop the registration belongs to.
reg   Registration to pause.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        The function paused reg, or reg was already
                    paused.
ccol_invalid_args   loop is CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID, or a handle that
                    is stale, or one that something already destroyed.
                    Or reg is CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID, or it fails to
                    resolve for another reason, such as a removal at
                    the same time. Or reg is a registration for a
                    queue or for a ccol_channel.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call at the same time as ccol_event_loop_remove(). Also safe to
call from inside a callback that runs on one of the reactor threads.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
/* Inside an on_readable callback, after the parse of a full set of
   request headers, and just before the connection goes to a worker
   thread for its own direct, blocking body I/O. reg is the handle that
   the callback gets: */
conn->reg = reg;
ccol_event_loop_pause(loop, reg);
if (ctpool_submit(worker_pool, process_request, conn, NULL) != ccol_success)
    ccol_event_loop_resume(loop, reg);  /* no worker: the reactor keeps it */
/* ... later, on the worker thread, after the server sends the
   response, when the connection is ready for its next request: */
ccol_event_loop_resume(loop, conn->reg);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_resume (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_add (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_remove (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_modify (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_reg_generation (3)
